# Setting up page wrappers

A dashboard has 3 wrappers, one inside another: the view, the section and the card. Home Assistant sets the view and the section. civica sets only the inside of its own cards.

| Wrapper | What it controls | Who sets it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| View | The greatest width of the dashboard, the space at the sides and how many sections fit side by side | Home Assistant, with `max_columns` in the view settings |
| Section | One column of the view, the gaps between sections and the grid inside | Home Assistant, with `column_span` in the section settings |
| Card | The edge of the card and the space inside it | The civica theme for the edge. The card itself for the space inside |

## The view

The view centres the sections on the screen. It is never wider than the sections it holds, at their greatest width, plus the gaps between them. On a wall panel wider than that, the extra space is shared at both sides.

The view adds space at the left and right, the same width as the gap between sections.

Set **Max sections side by side** (`max_columns`) to fewer than 4 to keep a wide screen to fewer columns. For example, set 2 on a hall wall panel so people read down 2 columns, not across 4.

Leave **Add additional space above** (`top_margin`) off. It adds 80px above the first sections to show more of the background, and pushes urgent cards down on a phone.

Leave **Dense section placement** (`dense_section_placement`) off. It moves sections into gaps, so the order is no longer the one people learned.

## The section

A section is one column of the view. A section can span more than one column. [Using the grid system](structure-grid.html#grid-and-columns) gives its sizes, the gaps around it and the grid inside it.

## The card

Every card sits in a card wrapper. The civica theme gives it square corners and a 1px border in the same colour as `civica-border-quiet`. [Section break](structure-section-break.html#surfaces-and-borders) owns when to add other borders.

A maintained custom card sets the space inside its own edge. The civica house lighting card uses 15px on narrow screens and 20px from 641px wide. [Spacing](structure-spacing.html) gives the full scale.

Do not change the native view or section spacing to make a dashboard look tighter. Every card that follows the native gaps lines up at every screen size.
